//! member: true
//!
//! std.term — the controlling terminal: whose turn it is, and how it reads.
//! Stability tier: core. The facade over wolf-lang's `[os.term]` (s219).
//!
//! **Whose turn.** One process group at a time is the terminal's
//! FOREGROUND: the group its Ctrl-C, Ctrl-\ and Ctrl-Z signal and the only
//! one that may read it. A job-control shell hands the foreground to a job
//! (`std.process.start_job` does it in the child, before the job runs) and
//! takes it back with `set_foreground(tty, group())` when the job ends.
//! The setters block `SIGTTOU` for their own duration, so a shell outside
//! the foreground is not stopped for taking its terminal back.
//!
//! **How it reads.** A `Mode` is the things an interactive program
//! changes: `canonical` (a line at a time, with the terminal's own
//! editing), `echo`, `signals` (Ctrl-C, Ctrl-\ and Ctrl-Z become signals),
//! `flow` (Ctrl-S and Ctrl-Q pause and resume output and never reach the
//! program), `extended` (the host's extended input: literal next, word
//! erase, reprint, discard), the three a terminal does not usually have
//! (`raw_cr`: a typed carriage return reaches the program as CR, not LF;
//! `raw_output`: a written LF reaches the terminal as LF, not CR LF;
//! `strip8`: input stripped to seven bits) and, when not canonical, how a
//! read waits — `min` bytes, or `time` tenths of a second. Every other
//! setting is left as found, so a mode read and set back restores exactly
//! what was changed. Save it, change it, put it back
//! (`term.set_mode(tty, saved)`); or, since wolf 0.2.34, register the put
//! back with the runtime (`restore_at_exit`), which then makes it on
//! every exit the runtime owns, a trap's included.
//!
//! **`flow` and `extended` before wolf 0.2.30.** The runtime gained the
//! two bits in wolf 0.2.30 (s234); from then a mode without them turns
//! flow control and extended input off. Before it the runtime never
//! reports them (`mode` answers both `false`) and refuses a mode that
//! carries them (`invalid`), and a set leaves both as found. A mode read
//! and set back is therefore exact on either side; a `Mode` built by hand
//! says what it wants of both.
//!
//! **`raw_cr`, `raw_output` and `strip8` before wolf 0.2.34.** The
//! runtime gained bits 5, 6 and 7 in wolf 0.2.34 (s248, ruling #97 R7),
//! each a setting the usual terminal does NOT have, so a 0 in each is
//! the usual setting: `ICRNL` on, `OPOST` on, `ISTRIP` off. From then a
//! mode without them writes the usual setting back where the terminal
//! had another (`stty -opost`); before it the runtime never reports them
//! (`mode` answers all three `false`), refuses a mode that carries them
//! (`invalid`) and leaves the three flags as found. A mode read and set
//! back through `mode`, `unpack` and `pack` is exact on either side, and
//! on a usual terminal every mode `pack` made before them packs the same
//! int.
//!
//! **What a line editor asks of it** (wolf 0.2.30, s234, ruling #73):
//! the window's `size` (and `signal.resize()` when it changes) and the
//! keys the user chose for erase, kill, interrupt and the rest (`chars`)
//! — spellings ruled #82. The wait that a key or a signal ends is
//! `signal.wait_fd` (ruling #82: the signal family's, and any descriptor
//! serves), so an editor blocks there instead of in a read a signal
//! cannot tear; since wolf 0.2.34 `signal.wait_fds` waits on the
//! terminal, a job's pipes and the signals at once, with a deadline
//! (the 25 ms that tells a lone ESC from an escape sequence). Nothing
//! here sets the special characters, and nothing restores the mode when
//! a program dies of a signal nobody listened for (no code of the
//! process runs then, the runtime's included).
//!
//! **Errors are rows.** A handle that is closed, forged or not a terminal
//! is `io`; a mode outside the bits is `invalid` (unreachable through
//! `Mode`, whose fields are clamped, but for `flow` or `extended` before
//! wolf 0.2.30 and the three bits before 0.2.34); every call is
//! `unsupported` on windows. lupin answers the shape rows and declines
//! every call that reaches the terminal by name; from lupin 0.1.56 its
//! shape takes bits 5–7 (0.1.55 refused them `invalid`, wolf-interp#268)
//! and `os_term_restore_at_exit` answers its shape and `io` rows.
//!
//! **A terminal for a program this one runs** (sc81, wolf 0.2.39's s292:
//! `[os.term.pty]`, `[os.term.size.set]`, `[os.term.attr]`). `open_pty`
//! makes a pseudo-terminal, a `Pty`: what the program on its `slave`
//! writes is read from its `master`, and what is written to the master is
//! the terminal's input, taken by its line discipline. `pty_name` is the
//! slave's name, `set_size` sets the window's size with RFC 4254's pixel
//! fields (the foreground group hears `signal.resize()` when any of the
//! four changes), and `attr` and `set_attr` read and set one setting by
//! its RFC 4254 opcode, as an SSH client sends them. A child takes the
//! slave as its controlling terminal with `open_controlling`, after
//! `std.process.lead_session`; `Pty` says the whole order, which is an
//! ssh server's answer to a `pty-req`. The master is an `fs.File`:
//! `signal.wait_fds` waits on it, `fs.set_nonblock` and `fs.write_some`
//! write to it without blocking, and `fs.read_chunk` reads it.
//!
//! **Capability:** `foreground`, `mode`, `set_mode`, `restore_at_exit`,
//! `size`, `chars`, `pty_name`, `set_size`, `attr` and `set_attr` are `io`
//! (they act on a descriptor the program holds, as `io.is_terminal`);
//! `set_foreground`, `open_pty` (with `process.pipe`: a pty is made to run
//! a program on) and `open_controlling` (it changes the process's
//! terminal) are `exec`, and `group` `env` (process context).
//! `pack`, `unpack`, `raw`, `raw_bytes`, `chars_from` and
//! `char_disabled` are pure, comptime-safe.

use std.fs

/// The terminal's mode, unpacked (`[os.term.mode]`). `raw_cr`,
/// `raw_output` and `strip8` (wolf 0.2.34) are each `false` on a usual
/// terminal: a typed Enter reads as LF, a written LF reaches the screen
/// as CR LF, and input keeps its eighth bit.
pub struct Mode {
    canonical: bool,
    echo: bool,
    signals: bool,
    flow: bool,
    extended: bool,
    raw_cr: bool,
    raw_output: bool,
    strip8: bool,
    min: int,
    time: int,
}

fn clamp(n: int) -> int {
    if n < 0 {
        return 0
    }
    if n > 255 {
        return 255
    }
    n
}

/// The packed int the builtin reads: canonical=1, echo=2, signals=4,
/// flow=8, extended=16, raw_cr=32, raw_output=64, strip8=128, `min` in
/// bits 8..15, `time` in bits 16..23 (`[os.term.mode]`).
/// `pack(unpack(v)) == v` for every mode the runtime reports, and a
/// `Mode` whose three new fields are `false` packs the int it packed
/// before wolf 0.2.34.
pub fn pack(m: Mode) -> int {
    var v = 0
    if m.canonical {
        v = v + 1
    }
    if m.echo {
        v = v + 2
    }
    if m.signals {
        v = v + 4
    }
    if m.flow {
        v = v + 8
    }
    if m.extended {
        v = v + 16
    }
    if m.raw_cr {
        v = v + 32
    }
    if m.raw_output {
        v = v + 64
    }
    if m.strip8 {
        v = v + 128
    }
    v + clamp(m.min) * 256 + clamp(m.time) * 65536
}

/// The `Mode` a packed int names.
pub fn unpack(v: int) -> Mode {
    Mode {
        canonical: v % 2 == 1,
        echo: v / 2 % 2 == 1,
        signals: v / 4 % 2 == 1,
        flow: v / 8 % 2 == 1,
        extended: v / 16 % 2 == 1,
        raw_cr: v / 32 % 2 == 1,
        raw_output: v / 64 % 2 == 1,
        strip8: v / 128 % 2 == 1,
        min: v / 256 % 256,
        time: v / 65536 % 256,
    }
}

/// The terminal's mode now.
pub fn mode(tty: fs.File) -> Mode ! {unsupported, io} {
    unpack(os_term_mode(tty.fd)?)
}

/// Set the terminal's mode (output already written drains first; input
/// already typed is kept).
pub fn set_mode(tty: fs.File, m: Mode) -> () ! {unsupported, invalid, io} {
    os_term_set_mode(tty.fd, pack(m))?
}

/// `m` with input a key at a time: not canonical, no echo, no flow
/// control and no extended input, one byte per read, no timer, so Ctrl-S,
/// Ctrl-Q, Ctrl-V and Ctrl-O arrive as keys, as `cfmakeraw(3)` leaves
/// them. `signals` is kept, so Ctrl-C still interrupts (ruling #81:
/// `raw` clears `flow` and `extended`). Before wolf 0.2.30 the runtime
/// cannot clear the two, so there they stay as found. `raw_cr`,
/// `raw_output` and `strip8` are kept as `m` has them, so `raw` sets on
/// every terminal what it set before wolf 0.2.34: Enter still reads as
/// LF and a written LF still reaches the screen as CR LF on a usual one
/// (`raw_bytes` turns both off).
pub fn raw(m: Mode) -> Mode {
    Mode {
        canonical: false,
        echo: false,
        signals: m.signals,
        flow: false,
        extended: false,
        raw_cr: m.raw_cr,
        raw_output: m.raw_output,
        strip8: m.strip8,
        min: 1,
        time: 0,
    }
}

/// `raw(m)` with every byte passed through untranslated, both ways:
/// `raw_cr` and `raw_output` on and `strip8` off, so a typed Enter reads
/// as CR (13), not LF (10, Ctrl-J's byte), a written LF reaches the
/// terminal alone, and input keeps its eighth bit. That is an editor's
/// raw mode, as C yew sets it (`cfmakeraw(3)`'s set as far as a `Mode`
/// reaches it; `signals` is kept, as `raw` keeps it, and an editor that
/// wants Ctrl-C as a key clears it). A program in this mode writes CR LF
/// where it means a new line. Pure and comptime-safe (sc69, ruling #97
/// R7: wolf 0.2.34's bits 5–7).
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// let cooked = term.unpack(1 + 2 + 4 + 8 + 16)
/// let r = term.raw_bytes(cooked)
/// r.raw_cr == true && r.raw_output == true && r.strip8 == false
/// term.pack(r) == 4 + 32 + 64 + 256
/// term.pack(term.raw(cooked)) == 4 + 256
/// ```
pub fn raw_bytes(m: Mode) -> Mode {
    let r = raw(m)
    Mode {
        canonical: r.canonical,
        echo: r.echo,
        signals: r.signals,
        flow: r.flow,
        extended: r.extended,
        raw_cr: true,
        raw_output: true,
        strip8: false,
        min: r.min,
        time: r.time,
    }
}

/// **s248 (`[os.term.restore]`, ruling #93 R1 C): put the terminal back
/// on every exit the runtime owns.** Registers, for this process, a
/// restore of `tty`: when the process ends — a return from `main`,
/// `process.exit`, an error from `main`, a trap (after its report), the
/// stack guard's report, `process.exit_signal` (before its raise), the
/// death of the proc root — the runtime sets `m` exactly as `set_mode`
/// sets it and then writes `bytes` to the terminal (an editor's
/// alternate-screen leave and cursor show). The restore runs in the
/// runtime, never in a handler of the program's. One registration a
/// process: the last call wins, and each restore runs once. The runtime
/// holds its own duplicate of the descriptor from the call, so a `tty`
/// the program closes first still restores, and the bytes are copied at
/// the call. Register the mode to come back to (the one `mode` read
/// before the program changed it); registering first and changing the
/// mode after is the usual order.
///
/// **What it does not cover:** a death by a signal nobody listened for —
/// `KILL`, a `TERM` or `HUP` under the default, a fault outside the stack
/// guard — runs no code of the process, so nothing is restored (listen
/// for `terminate()`, `reload()` and `quit()` and end through `exit`);
/// `process.exec` replaces the image and the registration with it; a
/// child a `process.fork` makes restores nothing.
///
/// Rows: `invalid` for a mode outside the bits (unreachable through
/// `Mode`); then `unsupported` on windows; then `io` for a handle that is
/// closed, forged or not a terminal, or a duplicate the host will not
/// make (ruling #97 R1). Never traps. The checked machine restores on
/// its own end, whatever the verdict (R8); lupin 0.1.56 answers the shape
/// and `io` rows and refuses a well-formed registration by name (0.1.55
/// did not resolve the call, wolf-interp#268).
///
/// Capability `io` (uncharged, as `set_mode`; ruling #97 R5); refused at
/// comptime. Delegates to `os_term_restore_at_exit`. The example is
/// prose, since a handle comes from `std.fs`: an editor reads `saved =
/// term.mode(tty)?`, registers `term.restore_at_exit(tty, saved,
/// leave)?` with the bytes that leave its alternate screen, then sets
/// `term.raw_bytes(saved)`; a trap no longer leaves the shell raw.
pub fn restore_at_exit(tty: fs.File, m: Mode, bytes: List[byte]) -> () ! {
    invalid,
    io,
    unsupported,
} {
    os_term_restore_at_exit(tty.fd, pack(m), bytes)?
}

/// The terminal's foreground process group.
pub fn foreground(tty: fs.File) -> int ! {unsupported, io} {
    os_term_foreground(tty.fd)?
}

/// Make `group_id` the terminal's foreground process group.
pub fn set_foreground(tty: fs.File, group_id: int) -> () ! {unsupported, io} {
    os_term_set_foreground(tty.fd, group_id)?
}

/// This process's own process group.
pub fn group() -> int ! {unsupported} {
    os_pgid()?
}

/// The terminal's window size (`[os.term.size]`): `rows` lines of
/// `columns` cells each.
pub struct Size {
    rows: int,
    columns: int,
}

/// The window's size as the terminal holds it: what the emulator last
/// told it, or what `stty rows … cols …` set (`TIOCGWINSZ`).
///
/// A size the terminal was never told is 0 (a pseudo-terminal nobody
/// sized): fall back on `COLUMNS` or 80, as without the call. When the
/// window changes, the foreground group hears `signal.resize()`, and the
/// next call answers the new size.
///
/// Rows: `io` for a handle that is closed, forged or not a terminal;
/// `unsupported` on windows. Never traps. lupin declines by name (it has
/// no `ioctl`), so the example is prose: `term.size(tty)?.columns` is the
/// width an editor lays a line out in.
///
/// Capability `io` (uncharged, as `mode`). Delegates to `os_term_size`.
/// Spelling ruled #82 (`Size`'s named fields over the builtin's pair).
pub fn size(tty: fs.File) -> Size ! {unsupported, io} {
    let (r, c) = os_term_size(tty.fd)?
    Size { rows: r, columns: c }
}

/// **s292 (`[os.term.size.set]`, sc81): set the window's size** of the
/// terminal `tty` names, as an emulator or `stty rows … cols …` does: `s`
/// is the rows and the columns `size` reads back, on either end of a
/// pseudo-terminal, and `xpixels` and `ypixels` the window's width and
/// height in pixels, RFC 4254's two other fields (0 when the client does
/// not know). The pixels are written at every call, so a server cannot
/// drop what a client sent: a change of the pixels alone signals too.
///
/// When any of the four changes, the host sends `signal.resize()` to the
/// terminal's foreground group (the program on a pty's slave), and the
/// same four again send nothing. An ssh server calls this on the master
/// for the `pty-req` and for each `window-change`.
///
/// Rows, in the order they are decided: `invalid` for any of the four
/// outside `0..65535`, before the handle is read; `unsupported` on
/// windows; `io` for a handle that is closed, forged or not a terminal.
/// Never traps. lupin 0.1.61 answers the shape and handle rows and
/// refuses a set on a terminal by name.
///
/// Capability `io` (uncharged, as `set_mode`); refused at comptime.
/// Delegates to `os_term_set_size`. The example is prose, since a handle
/// comes from `std.fs`: a `window-change` of 120 columns, 40 rows, 960 by
/// 640 pixels is `term.set_size(p.master, term.Size { rows: 40, columns:
/// 120 }, 960, 640)?`, and `term.size(p.slave)?.columns` is 120 after.
pub fn set_size(tty: fs.File, s: Size, xpixels: int, ypixels: int) -> () ! {
    invalid,
    io,
    unsupported,
} {
    os_term_set_size(tty.fd, s.rows, s.columns, xpixels, ypixels)?
}

/// A pseudo-terminal (`[os.term.pty]`): two `std.fs` files, the
/// `master`, which the program that made it keeps, and a `slave`, the
/// terminal another program runs on. Bytes written to the master are the
/// terminal's input, taken by its line discipline (echo, editing, the
/// signal characters); what the program writes to the slave is read from
/// the master. Both are close-on-exec: neither reaches a child except
/// through a descriptor map.
///
/// **Who closes what, in the order a server follows** (an ssh server's
/// answer to a `pty-req`, which `std.process.fork` and the calls here
/// make together):
///
/// 1. `let p = term.open_pty()?` and `let name = term.pty_name(p.master)?`.
/// 2. `term.set_size(p.master, …)` and `term.set_attr(p.master, op, v)`
///    for each mode the client sent (an op the host lacks is
///    `unsupported`; pass over it).
/// 3. `let f = process.fork()?`. **In the child:**
///    `process.lead_session()?`, then `var tty = term.open_controlling(name)?`,
///    then the program with `tty` on 0, 1 and 2 (`process.set_stdin`,
///    `set_stdout`, `set_stderr`) by `process.exec`. The child keeps the
///    slave it inherited until its own open (macOS puts a terminal's size
///    and settings back when its slave is opened while no descriptor of it
///    is open); the exec closes the inherited master and slave, being
///    close-on-exec.
/// 4. **In the parent:** `fs.close(take p.slave)?` after the fork, then
///    relay the master: `signal.wait_fds` waits on it, `fs.set_nonblock`
///    with `fs.write_some` writes what it takes, `fs.read_chunk` reads what
///    is there; `set_size` at each window change; `fs.close(take
///    p.master)` at the end, which hangs the terminal up for the program.
///
/// **The end, read on the master.** Once every descriptor of the slave is
/// closed (the program and everything it started have ended), a read of
/// the master answers `io` on linux (the host's `EIO`, 5 in `os_error()`)
/// and `eof` on macOS; either ends the relay. After the master's last
/// close the slave's name is gone.
pub struct Pty {
    master: fs.File,
    slave: fs.File,
}

/// **s292 (`[os.term.pty]`, sc81): a new pseudo-terminal**, its master and
/// a slave, both close-on-exec; the slave granted and unlocked, and
/// neither end this process's controlling terminal, whatever it leads.
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` on windows, by name; `io` for a host that cannot
/// make one (no `/dev/ptmx`, none free, the descriptor limit), with the
/// host's number in `os_error()`, and nothing left open. Never traps.
/// The checked machine opens the pair in the `wolf` process. lupin 0.1.61
/// refuses the call by name (Rust's standard library has no
/// `posix_openpt`), so the example is prose: `let p = term.open_pty()?`,
/// then `term.pty_name(p.master)?` is `/dev/pts/N` on linux.
///
/// Capability `exec` (with `process.pipe`: a pty is made to run a program
/// on); refused at comptime. Delegates to `os_term_open_pty`.
pub fn open_pty() -> Pty ! {io, unsupported} {
    let (m, s) = os_term_open_pty()?
    Pty { master: fs.File { fd: m }, slave: fs.File { fd: s } }
}

/// **s292 (`[os.term.pty]`, sc81): the slave's name** for the master
/// `master` names: `/dev/pts/N` on linux and PAX, `/dev/ttysN` on macOS.
/// A child opens it by this name to take it as its controlling terminal
/// (`open_controlling`).
///
/// Rows: `unsupported` on windows; `io` for a handle that is closed,
/// forged or not a master (a file, a slave, another terminal). Never
/// traps. lupin 0.1.61 answers a closed or forged handle's `io` and
/// refuses the rest by name.
///
/// Capability `io` (uncharged, with `mode`); refused at comptime.
/// Delegates to `os_term_pty_name`. The example is prose, since a handle
/// comes from `std.fs` or `open_pty`: `term.pty_name(p.master)?` names the
/// slave, and `term.pty_name(p.slave)` is `io`.
pub fn pty_name(master: fs.File) -> str ! {io, unsupported} {
    os_term_pty_name(master.fd)?
}

/// **s292 (`[os.term.attr]`, sc81): one setting of the terminal `tty`
/// names, by its RFC 4254 section 8 opcode** (with RFC 8160's 42, IUTF8):
/// a special character, 1 to 18, as its byte or -1 for disabled (as
/// `chars` reads it); a flag (30 to 42 input, 50 to 62 local, 70 to 75
/// output, 92 and 93 control) as 0 or 1; the character size, 90 CS7 and 91
/// CS8, 1 for the size the terminal has; the speeds, 128 input and 129
/// output, in bits per second. The opcode is the published number an SSH
/// client sends, and it is the vocabulary here.
///
/// Rows, in the order they are decided: `invalid` for an opcode outside
/// the vocabulary (0, 19..29, 43..49, 63..69, 76..89, 94..127, 130 and up,
/// a negative); `unsupported` on windows, for an op the host's terminal
/// driver lacks (linux 11, 15 and 17; macOS 15, 16, 37, 52 and 71) and for
/// a speed the runtime's table does not name; `io` for a handle that is
/// closed, forged or not a terminal. Never traps. lupin 0.1.61 answers
/// every row decided before a terminal is reached and refuses the rest by
/// name.
///
/// Capability `io` (uncharged, as `mode`); refused at comptime. Delegates
/// to `os_term_attr`. The example is prose, since a handle comes from
/// `std.fs` or `open_pty`: `term.attr(p.slave, 53)?` is 1 while the
/// terminal echoes, and `term.attr(p.slave, 19)` is `invalid` on every
/// host.
pub fn attr(tty: fs.File, op: int) -> int ! {invalid, io, unsupported} {
    os_term_attr(tty.fd, op)?
}

/// **s292 (`[os.term.attr]`, sc81): set one setting of the terminal `tty`
/// names, by its RFC 4254 opcode**, every other field as found (output
/// already written drains first, as `set_mode`). `value` is in the op's
/// range (`attr` names them): a character -1..255 (a byte equal to the
/// host's disabled value disables it too), a flag 0 or 1, a size 0 or 1
/// (1 selects it; 0 leaves the size as found, since a client sends both),
/// a speed in bits per second from the runtime's table (0, 50, 75, 110,
/// 134, 150, 200, 300, 600, 1200, 1800, 2400, 4800, 9600, 19200, 38400,
/// 57600, 115200, 230400). The setting is read back after the set, so an
/// answer of `()` means the terminal holds what was asked.
///
/// Rows, in the order they are decided: `invalid` for an opcode outside
/// the vocabulary or a value outside its op's range; `unsupported` on
/// windows, for an op the host lacks, for a speed not in the table, and
/// for a set the host did not keep (linux's pseudo-terminal keeps neither
/// CS7 nor PARENB; macOS keeps a set PENDIN), with every other field as it
/// was; `io` for a handle that is closed, forged or not a terminal, and
/// for any other refusal of the host's. Never traps. A server applying a
/// client's modes passes over `unsupported`, as `sshd` does. lupin 0.1.61
/// answers every row decided before a terminal is reached and refuses the
/// rest by name.
///
/// Capability `io` (uncharged, as `set_mode`); refused at comptime.
/// Delegates to `os_term_set_attr`. The example is prose, since a handle
/// comes from `std.fs` or `open_pty`: a client's `ECHO 0` is
/// `term.set_attr(p.master, 53, 0)?`, and `term.set_attr(p.master, 53, 2)`
/// is `invalid` on every host.
pub fn set_attr(tty: fs.File, op: int, value: int) -> () ! {invalid, io, unsupported} {
    os_term_set_attr(tty.fd, op, value)?
}

/// **sc81: open the terminal `name` as THIS process's controlling
/// terminal** — the step a pty's program takes in its own session, after
/// `std.process.lead_session`, so that the terminal's interrupt, quit and
/// suspend keys, its hangup and `signal.resize()` reach it and `/dev/tty`
/// opens (`[os.term.pty]`, `[os.term.session]`). The answer is the
/// terminal, open for reading and writing, to be placed on 0, 1 and 2
/// for the program's exec; it is this process's controlling terminal, with
/// this process's group its foreground.
///
/// It is the open the runtime makes without `O_NOCTTY`: a session leader
/// with no controlling terminal that opens a terminal no session holds
/// takes it. The call checks that it did, because nothing else would say
/// so: an open made before `lead_session`, or from a process that leads
/// none, or of a terminal another session holds, gives a terminal that is
/// not the controlling one, and a program on it gets no job control and no
/// keys' signals.
///
/// Rows, in the order they are decided: `unsupported` on windows, before
/// the name is looked at; `not_found` for a name that does not exist;
/// `io` for one that is not a character device, without opening it (so
/// nothing is created); `denied` for an open the host refuses; `io` for
/// one that is not a terminal, or did not become this process's
/// controlling terminal with its group in the foreground — the file is
/// closed then, and the terminal is not held. Never traps. The checked
/// machine opens in the `wolf` process. lupin 0.1.61 refuses the call by
/// name (at `os_pgid`).
///
/// Capability `exec` (it changes the process's terminal, as
/// `set_foreground`); refused at comptime. Delegates to `fs_stat`,
/// `fs_open_mode` (mode 3), `os_isatty`, `os_term_foreground` and
/// `os_pgid`. The example is prose, since the call that succeeds takes a
/// terminal: in a fork's child, `process.lead_session()?`, then
/// `var tty = term.open_controlling(name)?` and `term.foreground(tty)? ==
/// term.group()?`.
pub fn open_controlling(name: str) -> fs.File ! {denied, io, not_found, unsupported} {
    let g = os_pgid()?
    let info = fs.stat(name)?
    if info.kind != 6 {
        return io
    }
    let fd = fs_open_mode(name, 3) else |e| match e {
        not_found => { return not_found },
        denied => { return denied },
        exists => { return io },
        invalid => { return io },
        io => { return io },
    }
    let is_tty = os_isatty(fd) else false
    var fg = -1
    if is_tty {
        fg = os_term_foreground(fd) else -1
    }
    if fg != g {
        fs_close(fd) else {}
        return io
    }
    fs.File { fd: fd }
}

/// The terminal's special characters (`[os.term.chars]`): the byte the
/// user chose for each (`stty erase ^H`), or `char_disabled()` for one
/// that is disabled (`stty intr undef`). `word_erase`, `literal_next`,
/// `reprint` and `discard` are not POSIX's; both hosts have them, and
/// they act only with `Mode.extended` on.
pub struct Chars {
    erase: int,
    kill: int,
    interrupt: int,
    quit: int,
    suspend: int,
    end_of_file: int,
    word_erase: int,
    literal_next: int,
    start: int,
    stop: int,
    reprint: int,
    discard: int,
}

/// The word `Chars` holds for a character that is disabled: -1, the
/// builtin's own (`[os.term.chars]`; the host's disabled value never
/// reaches the program). Total and pure.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// term.char_disabled() == 0 - 1
/// ```
pub fn char_disabled() -> int {
    -1
}

fn char_at(v: List[int], i: int) -> int {
    var k = 0
    for c in v {
        if k == i {
            return c
        }
        k = k + 1
    }
    -1
}

/// The `Chars` a list in `[os.term.chars]`'s order names: erase, kill,
/// interrupt, quit, suspend, end of file, word erase, literal next,
/// start, stop, reprint, discard. An entry the list does not reach is
/// `char_disabled()`; entries past the twelfth are not read (the
/// builtin's list grows only at its end). Total and pure.
///
/// ```wolf-doc-example
/// var v = List[int]()
/// (mut v).push(127)
/// (mut v).push(21)
/// (mut v).push(3)
/// let c = term.chars_from(v)
/// c.erase == 127 && c.interrupt == 3 && c.discard == term.char_disabled()
/// ```
pub fn chars_from(v: List[int]) -> Chars {
    Chars {
        erase: char_at(v, 0),
        kill: char_at(v, 1),
        interrupt: char_at(v, 2),
        quit: char_at(v, 3),
        suspend: char_at(v, 4),
        end_of_file: char_at(v, 5),
        word_erase: char_at(v, 6),
        literal_next: char_at(v, 7),
        start: char_at(v, 8),
        stop: char_at(v, 9),
        reprint: char_at(v, 10),
        discard: char_at(v, 11),
    }
}

/// The terminal's special characters now. A line editor that turns
/// `canonical` and `signals` off reads these first, so erase, kill,
/// interrupt and end of file keep the keys the user set. Nothing here
/// sets them (`stty` does).
///
/// Rows: `io` for a handle that is closed, forged or not a terminal;
/// `unsupported` on windows. Never traps. lupin declines by name (it has
/// no `tcgetattr`); the example is prose: `term.chars(tty)?.erase` is
/// the key that deletes a character.
///
/// Capability `io` (uncharged, as `mode`). Delegates to `os_term_chars`.
/// Spelling ruled #82 (`Chars`' named fields, with `chars_from` and
/// `char_disabled`).
pub fn chars(tty: fs.File) -> Chars ! {unsupported, io} {
    chars_from(os_term_chars(tty.fd)?)
}
